## Diagnosis direction (verify, do not assume)
The replay policy applies while the *same* target route may come back (transient disconnect / handover). When the fixed target's lifecycle is
terminal (peer process gone: mesh peer removed / lifecycle generation retired / probe says crashed), 08-routing ends the current operation
`Unavailable` at once — no replay, no wait for the deadline. Today (a) `ZLinkDurableRequest` replays regardless of the target lifecycle, and
(b) the join (`ZLinkActorRemoteJoiner.cs:1046`) runs its own deadline/cancellation in parallel to the sender's deadline, so the join reports
DeadlineExceeded/TaskCanceledException before the sender's exhaustion kind is ever produced. Two deadline owners and a missing lifecycle-terminal
rule. **Parity first**: read how node (`framework/languages/node/packages/framework/src/runtime/...` durable request/replay + actor remote join)
and java (`ZLinkJavaDurableRequest.java`, `ZLinkActorRemoteJoiner`-equivalent) end this exact G4 case with `Unavailable` — both languages pass
ZoneWorld G4 on the same Core — and make dotnet follow the same rule set (which layer observes the target lifecycle, who owns the deadline).

## Fix (single owner)
- One deadline owner for a durable operation: the operation (sender) owns the remaining deadline; callers await its terminal and do not race a
  second timer. Remove the competing cancellation, do not add a third.
- One lifecycle-terminal rule: when the fixed target's lifecycle ends, the operation ends `Unavailable` immediately (spec row); replay only while
  the route may return. Implement where node/java implement it (do not add a dotnet-only state machine).
- Regression tests at that layer: (1) admitted request → pair terminated → target lifecycle terminal → `Unavailable` promptly, no replay after the
  terminal; (2) transient disconnect (lifecycle alive) → replay continues per policy; (3) exhaustion kinds unchanged. Keep existing
  `DurableSenderRuntimeTests` assertions.
- State rule count before/after.

